GLOBAL
ALWAYS
* Follow KISS, SOLID, DRY, YAGNI.
* Ask clarifying questions when context is missing; assume nothing.
* Keep output minimal, idiomatic, and production‑ready.
* Follow instructions, sticking to the scope of the task
* 
NEVER
* Invent requirements.
* Execute shell commands unless explicitly told to.
* Overengineer
* Go beyond the scope of the instructions

1. Architecture & Project Structure (Use the src/ layout, one package per bounded context, and a high‑level README per domain when requested.)
ALWAYS
* Place all application code under src/ or a top‑level package.
* One responsibility per module; one bounded context per package.
* Keep infrastructure adapters (DB, HTTP, cache) in /infra; domain models in /domain; FastAPI routers in /api.
* Store configuration in environment variables or dedicated files (.env, pyproject.toml).
* Maintain clear documentation inside the code (docstrings, comments). Avoid separate .md documents unless explicitly requested.
NEVER
* Mix unrelated concerns or drop code at the repository root.
* Hard‑code credentials or environment‑specific settings.

2. Code Style & Documentation (Type hints + PEP 257 docstrings; sorted imports; no “god objects”)
ALWAYS
* Use typing and Pydantic models for all I/O boundaries.
* Write precise docstrings covering purpose, parameters, returns, raises, and usage examples.
* Order imports: standard library → third‑party → local.
* Fail fast: raise typed exceptions, log with contextual data, return appropriate 4xx/5xx HTTP codes.
NEVER
* Create circular imports or giant “god” classes/functions.
* Swallow exceptions silently.
* Rely on external markdown documents for core documentation; the code should be self‑documenting.

3. Data Layer (SQLAlchemy 2.x) — Session‑per‑request + Unit‑of‑Work ⇒ atomic transactions & easy rollbacks
ALWAYS
* Keep all database logic in database.py; other code imports it but never re‑implements DB details.
* Create a module‑level engine (via create_async_engine) and an async_sessionmaker; these singletons are safe.
* Provide a get_session() async context manager (or FastAPI dependency) that yields a fresh AsyncSession per request, commits on success, rolls back on error, and always closes.
* Encapsulate writes inside a Unit‑of‑Work pattern so each business action is wrapped in one transaction.
* Declare models with the SQLAlchemy 2.0 declarative API; map only the fields you truly need.
* Use SQLAlchemy Core queries (select(), update(), etc.); never interpolate raw SQL strings.
* Leverage type hints and Pydantic schemas at I/O boundaries for validation and clarity.
* Log queries at debug level when troubleshooting (e.g., echo=True via an env flag), but keep it off in production.
NEVER
* Expose a live Session/AsyncSession as a global mutable variable; only the engine and sessionmaker live at module scope.
* Rely on implicit autocommit; always commit/rollback explicitly inside get_session() or a Unit‑of‑Work.
* Mutate ORM objects outside an active session scope.
* Execute any database query or transaction logic outside database.py (repositories and services should call its helpers).

4. API Layer (FastAPI + Pydantic v2) (End‑to‑end async, validated, paginated, OpenAPI‑documented)
ALWAYS
* Define every handler as async def.
* Use response\_model and explicit status\_code.
* Validate inputs with Pydantic v2 models; use Settings for secrets.
* Implement pagination, filtering, and sorting on list endpoints.
NEVER
* Block the event loop with CPU‑bound tasks; off‑load to background workers.
* Return unvalidated dicts or ORM instances directly.

5. AI / LLM / Agent Layer (LangGraph / LangChain) (Moderation, Redis cache, streaming, cost control)
ALWAYS
* Keep prompts in separate template files; version‑control them.
* Have a ai.py file that handles AI / LLM function calling, whether it is using Lang calls or direct API calls.
* Use LangGraph nodes as pure functions; unit‑test them in isolation.
* Enable Redis‑based caching to deduplicate calls; expire caches on model upgrades.
* Stream responses when supported; implement exponential backoff on 429 errors.
* Insert human‑in‑the‑loop checkpoints for high‑risk operations.
* Log and track token usage.
NEVER
* Hard‑code API keys; always load from a secret manager.
* Send PII or internal prompts in telemetry.
* Use AI calls 

6. Async & Performance (Leverage asyncio 3.12/3.13 and FastAPI concurrency patterns)

ALWAYS
* Use async/await for all I/O; off‑load blocking code with run\_in\_threadpool.
* Batch external API calls; throttle via an async rate limiter.
* Profile with asyncio.run(main, debug=True) and measure p95 latency.
NEVER
* Mix synchronous and asynchronous code in the same call chain without off‑loading.
* Await CPU‑bound work on the event loop.

7. Logging & Observability (Structured JSON logs + contextvars; ready for ELK/Sentry)

ALWAYS
* Focus on UX/UI. Readibility is king when it comes to logging.
* Configure structlog or logging with python‑json‑logger at application start.
* Enrich logs with request\_id, user\_id, route, and latency.
* Emit DEBUG/INFO for normal flow, WARNING for recoverable issues, ERROR for failures.
* Use emojis when they help.
NEVER
* Log secrets, tokens, or raw user messages.
* Swallow errors or use bare except: blocks.

8. Security & Environment (Secure keys, OWASP API Top‑10, principle of least privilege)
ALWAYS
* Load secrets via environment variables or Pydantic Settings; rotate keys periodically.
* Validate auth tokens early; return 401/403 promptly.
* Keep dependencies and OS packages updated; run pip‑audit in CI.
* Use SSH key authentication for servers; configure firewalls (UFW) and Fail2Ban.
* Run containers and services as non‑root wherever possible.
NEVER
* Disable TLS, CSRF, or authentication for convenience.
* Expose internal stack traces or raw database errors.

9. Testing (Only when explicitly requested; prefer command‑line tests)
ALWAYS (when tests are requested)
* Follow TDD: write a failing test, implement the code, then refactor.
* Run tests via the command line (pytest); isolate DB tests with pytest‑asyncio and transactional rollbacks.
* Mark slow integration tests and parallelize with pytest‑xdist.
NEVER
* Auto‑generate boilerplate tests without a prompt.
* Enter endless test‑fix loops that over‑engineer solutions.

10. Deployment & Containers (Multi‑stage builds, minimal attack surface; applies when deployment files are requested)
ALWAYS
* Use python\:slim base images; install dependencies with --no-cache-dir; run as non‑root.
* Include a .dockerignore and mount secrets at runtime.
* Employ rolling updates or start‑first strategies to minimize downtime.
NEVER
* Ship development tooling layers in production images.
* Run containers in privileged mode unless absolutely necessary.

11. Dependency Management
ALWAYS
* Pin versions in pyproject.toml; run pip‑compile --upgrade weekly via CI.
* Remove unused packages promptly; audit with OWASP Dependency‑Check.
NEVER
* Depend on abandoned or unverifiable libraries.
* Upgrade dependencies blindly without reviewing changelogs and CVEs.

12. Prompt Engineering & AI Collaboration (Modular prompts, minimal few‑shot, token‑cost metrics)
ALWAYS
* Keep prompts short, declarative, and context‑rich; use {{variable}} syntax.
* Track token usage per endpoint; alert when cost exceeds baseline.
* Version prompts alongside code; write tests with synthetic inputs.
NEVER
* Rely solely on one‑shot prompts for critical paths.
* Combine multiple concerns in one giant prompt.
